Cab Driver Website: Research and Prompts
1. Market research: what converts on a cab driver’s site

Most customers in India find a driver through Google Maps, WhatsApp, or a local search like “Modinagar to Haridwar taxi”. They decide in about 10 seconds. The site has to answer three questions fast: Can I trust him? What will it cost? How do I reach him right now?

Must-have elements

Sticky Call Now and WhatsApp buttons on every screen. On mobile, make them a bottom bar. This is the single most important element.
Hero section with the driver’s photo, name, city, “24x7 Available”, and a call button.
Trust signals: years of experience, number of trips, verified or police-verified badge, Google rating, customer reviews, car photos, and registration, permit, and insurance details.
Fleet: Sedan (Dzire, Etios), SUV (Ertiga, Innova), and Tempo Traveller if available. Show seats, luggage capacity, AC, and rate per km.
Services: local city rides, airport transfer (Delhi IGI), railway station pickup, outstation one-way and round trip, Haridwar-Rishikesh pilgrimage trips, Kanwar Yatra, wedding and event cars, and monthly or corporate bookings.
Popular routes with fixed or estimated fares: Modinagar, Ghaziabad, Meerut, Delhi, Noida, Gurugram, Haridwar, and All India.
Distance and fare estimator (the feature you requested).
Simple booking form that opens WhatsApp or a call with details pre-filled.
Reviews, FAQ (toll, parking, night charges, waiting charges, cancellation), Hindi and English toggle, and a footer with address, hours, and Google Maps embed.
Local SEO: separate landing pages for routes such as “Modinagar to Haridwar Taxi” and “Ghaziabad to Delhi Airport Cab”. Local drivers get most of their leads this way.

What to avoid: long forms, forced signup, heavy animations, small tap targets, and hidden pricing. Customers leave when they can’t see a price or a phone number.

