4. Prompt 2: Distance (km) and fare feature

Use this in your code-building AI (Cursor, Claude Code, Bolt, Lovable):

Build a "Distance & Fare Calculator" component for a taxi website.

FUNCTIONALITY:
- Two inputs: "Pickup location" and "Drop location" with live autocomplete.
- Autocomplete must prioritize these places: Modinagar, Ghaziabad,
  Gurugram, Delhi, Noida, Haridwar, Meerut, and also support any city,
  town, or address across India (restrict country to IN).
- On submit, calculate REAL ROAD distance (not straight-line) and travel
  duration using a real routing API.
- Show: distance in km, estimated duration, estimated fare, and a route
  preview on a map with pickup and drop markers.
- Options: car type (Sedan/SUV/Innova/Tempo Traveller) and trip type
  (One-way / Round trip).
- Fare formula (configurable in a single config file, drivers edit rates):
    one-way fare  = max(distance_km * rate_per_km[car], minimum_fare[car])
    round-trip    = max(distance_km * 2, min_daily_km * days) * rate_per_km
    + driver_allowance (if overnight) + night_charge (if 10pm-6am)
  Show "Toll and parking extra as per actual" as a note.
- Result card has two CTAs: "Book on WhatsApp" (pre-filled message with
  pickup, drop, km, fare, car type) and "Call Now" (tel: link).

DATA SOURCE (choose one and implement):
  Option A (recommended for production): Google Maps Platform, using the
  Places Autocomplete API plus the Routes/Directions API
  (needs an API key and billing).
  Option B (free): OpenStreetMap Nominatim for geocoding (restrict to
  countrycodes=in) plus OSRM or OpenRouteService for road distance.
  Use Leaflet for the map. Respect usage policies, add caching and
  debounce (400 ms).

TECH: Next.js (App Router) + TypeScript + Tailwind. Put API calls in
server routes (/api/distance) so keys stay hidden. Validate inputs, handle
errors (location not found, API down), show loading states, round km to
1 decimal, and cache repeated routes.

QUICK-PICK: show chips for common routes (Modinagar→Delhi,
Modinagar→Haridwar, Ghaziabad→Meerut, Noida→Gurugram, Delhi→Haridwar)
that auto-fill the form.

Distances from the API are real road distances. Fares are estimates, so the driver should set his own per-km rates. I have not looked up current market rates, so use placeholders until he decides.

